<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lale Andersen's English recording, words by Norman Baillie-Stewart</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lina Termini's Italian recording, words by Nino Rastelli</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lily of the Lamplight (Tommie Connor)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The D-Day Dodgers (Harry Pynn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marlene Dietrich's recordings for the OSS and for Decca</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944–1945</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Perry Como</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (No. 13 in the United States)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The True Story of Lili Marlene, film (Humphrey Jennings)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">film about the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lili Marleen, film (Rainer Werner Fassbinder)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980</span><span class="sg-chain-h">film about Lale Andersen and the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Judgment at Nuremberg, film</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">March of the Siliwangi Division, Indonesian Army</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1948</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Parody by Lev Kopelev, and Joseph Brodsky's Russian poem based on it</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lili Marleen (Amanda Lear)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Das Lied eines jungen Soldaten auf der Wacht, poem by Hans Leip</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1915 (published 1937)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words</span></li></ul></section>
